{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTLcexPwArPLMjcBZNwH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`test_large_sequence` fails because the test and the code disagree about what happens when the sequence number has more than four digits.\n\n- `invoice.py:5` uses `f\"INV-{year}-{seq:04}\"`. In Python, `0004` is a minimum width, not a cap. So `12345` formats as `INV-2026-12345`.\n- `test_invoice.py:11` expects `INV-2026-2345`, which keeps only the last four digits.\n\nRunning `python3 -m unittest test_invoice -v` confirms it:\n\n```\nAssertionError: 'INV-2026-12345' != 'INV-2026-2345'\n```\n\n`test_format` passes. Only the overflow case fails.\n\nThe repo doesn't say which behavior is intended, and I did not change anything. The two fixes mean different things:\n\n- **Change the test** to expect `INV-2026-12345`. This is the behavior the code has. Invoice numbers stay unique, but they are no longer always four digits.\n- **Change the code** to `f\"INV-{year}-{seq % 10000:04}\"`, which makes the test pass as written. This wraps the sequence, so invoice 12345 gets the same number as invoice 2345. That can produce duplicate invoice numbers, which is usually a serious problem for invoices.\n\nMy recommendation is to change the test, unless your rules require a fixed four-digit width. Which one do you want? Once you decide, I'll make the change and run the suite."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":577,"cache_read_input_tokens":39809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":577},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60ad7aa3-3ade-4173-9829-d267599ba6fd","uuid":"ada5dfd4-6e9e-4f8a-a4a7-1c66285434ff","timestamp":"2026-